    "result_text": "Both tests pass. The change is complete and the suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI made the `householdId` deterministic and reworked household duplicate handling to match the spec.\n\n**Core derivation** \u2014 `Households.java`\n- `idFor(lastName, postcode)` now hashes `normalize(lastName) + \"|\" + postcode` and returns the **first 12 hex chars** (6 bytes) of SHA-256 (was 16 hex over lastName + street address).\n\n**Assignment** \u2014 `AssignHousehold.java`\n- Now assigns the deterministic `householdId` to **every** owner from `(lastName, postcode)`. Removed the `sharesHousehold` gate and the back-fill loop \u2014 since the id is a pure function of `(lastName, postcode)`, owners in the same household map to the same value automatically.\n\n**Duplicate block** \u2014 `RequireUniqueIdentity.java`\n- Now a household check: if an existing owner already has the request's computed `householdId`, it throws `DuplicateIdentityException` \u2192 **409**. Setting `sharesHousehold` bypasses the block entirely (created as a declared member). Owners in different households derive different ids and never collide.\n\n**Possible-duplicate flag** \u2014 `AssignPossibleDuplicate.java`\n- A declared member (`sharesHousehold=true`) is never flagged (`possibleDuplicate=false`); a declared member is not a suspected duplicate. Other cases keep the lastName+postcode soft-match logic.\n\n**Supporting** \u2014 `IdentityKeys.java` (dropped the now-unused `forRequest`; `forOwner` still exposes the key via the mapper, with `householdId` always populated) and `DuplicateIdentityException.java` (message/doc reworded to household terms). `AssignHouseholdSize` needed no change \u2014 it already keys off `householdId`, which is now the computed value.\n\n**Tests** \u2014 the existing `coreSameLastNameAndPostcodeShareHouseholdId` still passes, and I added `householdDuplicateRejectedUnlessDeclared` verifying the 409, the `sharesHousehold` bypass, and that the declared member is not flagged. `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec mentions \"the household-size input to the membership level.\" In this codebase `householdSize` is computed (and now correctly keys off the deterministic `householdId`) but `MembershipLevels` does not actually consume it \u2014 nothing wires household size into the level. I treated the phrase as identifying a household consumer that must use the computed id (which `AssignHouseholdSize` now does) rather than a request to newly feed size into the level, since no test or existing code establishes that link. Let me know if you intended membership level to actually depend on household size.",
